#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""witness-tap — retention + queue + manifest + heartbeat for the Monad witness seam.

The Monad replay (`run_replay.py --zkvm-witness <dir>`) dumps a witness for EVERY block it executes:
~7 MB each, ~5 blocks/min at the tip = ~2 GB/h, and it never cleans up. This is the loop that turns that
firehose into the three things the RTP pipeline actually needs, and nothing else:

  1. QUEUE   — frame every block (the RTP target: blocks as they arrive, NOT a ÷100 sample) straight
               from the dump dir into the prover queue, via the guest's own gen-inputs --src-dir:
               <dump>/<block>.witness -> <repo>/guests/monad-zisk/fixtures/1-<block>.bin  (+ its root)
               No in-repo staging copy: at one 7 MB witness per 12 s slot that would double disk and I/O
               for nothing. Set PROVE_EVERY=100 for the ethproofs SAMPLING cadence instead (cheap
               benchmark mode); 1 (the default) is real-time.
  2. PRUNE   — the RAW witness is deleted the moment it is framed. It is strictly redundant: the framed
               input is `LE64(len) + witness + pad`, so it CONTAINS the witness verbatim and the original is
               recoverable by dropping 8 bytes. Holding both meant 14 MB per block across two directories
               for nothing. The dump dir therefore drains every cycle (watch `raw=` in the log: it should
               sit at 0-1), and retention acts on the framed input alone.
               A witness younger than SETTLE_SECS is left for the next cycle — framing a half-written one
               used to be recoverable from the raw copy, and no longer is.

               A witness exists to be proved, so its input is dropped once the proof is in hand. Retention
               is driven by run-data/prove-farm.csv (the same source its own `is_proven` uses), not by a blind
               ring, and a block is KEPT when it is still interesting:

                 proved ok        -> drop the framed input; the proof is the artefact now
                 proof FAILED     -> keep, that is exactly what needs re-proving or debugging
                 never proved     -> keep until it is SKIP_AFTER blocks behind the tip, then drop as
                                     abandoned (with --newest-first the prover never comes back for it);
                                     the abandoned count IS the coverage gap, so it is logged, not hidden
                 sample block     -> keep forever if block % KEEP_SAMPLE == 0 — the reproducible corpus
                                     the cross-zkVM comparison in this repo needs
                 outlier          -> keep if the witness is >= KEEP_BIG_MB or the proof took
                                     >= KEEP_SLOW_SECS: the heavy blocks are the ones worth profiling
                 newest KEEP_RECENT -> always kept, so nothing in flight is pulled from under a prover

               KEEPING a block and OFFERING it to the prover are different decisions. A block kept for
               interest stays in the queue as an artefact, but `in_window` stops it from being framed in the
               first place once it is SKIP_AFTER behind, and cli/prove-farm's own is_proven skips it once
               proved — so an outlier from 280 blocks ago never burns a proof slot.

               `*.exec-report.json` files are NEVER deleted: a few hundred bytes each, and they carry the
               deterministic work-unit (ZisK steps) for a block whose 7 MB witness is long gone.
  3. RECORD  — one manifest row per block with the timestamps the latency report joins on, plus a
               heartbeat to the ethproofs mock (POST /status) so the dashboard knows the chain tip.

Queueing every block while the prover can only take one every ~20–70 s is deliberate: --newest-first
then always proves the freshest available block, and the fraction of blocks actually proved becomes the
honest RTP metric (coverage), reported by profiling/rtp-latency.py alongside per-block latency.

Timestamps (manifest.csv). Everything except t_block is this box's clock — deliberately, so pipeline
latency is measured on ONE clock and never mixes in the laptop's:

  block            the block number
  t_block          the block header's own timestamp, from the RPC (chain time; empty for non-promoted)
  t_avail          mtime of the block_db file — when the fetcher had the block locally
  t_witness        mtime of the .witness — when the replay finished executing the block
  t_queued         when the framed input landed in the prover queue
  bytes            witness size
  post_state_root  the expected root, the anchor for `run verify EXPECTED_ROOT=…`
  promoted         1 if it was framed into the queue, 0 if it was not (too far behind)
  block_hash       the header hash, from the same RPC call as t_block (empty for non-promoted). With the
                   two roots it completes the ground truth for cli/check-pv, which verifies every value the
                   guest commits before a proof is submitted.

Deliberately NOT here: proving and submission timings. Those come from prove-farm's report.json and the
mock's submissions.jsonl; joining the three is the latency report, not this loop's job.

Config (env, all optional except the paths' defaults being sane on the devcore box):
  WITNESS_DIR=~/witnesses      REPO=~/zkvm-bench        BLOCK_DB=~/block_db
  RPC=http://chi-001.devcore4.com:8545                  MANIFEST=<WITNESS_DIR>/../witness-manifest.csv
  PROVE_EVERY=1 (RTP; 100 = ethproofs sampling)       INTERVAL=6
  KEEP_RECENT=10 (never touched, may be in flight)      SKIP_AFTER=100 (un-proved -> abandoned)
  KEEP_SAMPLE=1000 (benchmark corpus)   KEEP_BIG_MB=14   KEEP_SLOW_SECS=45  (outliers worth profiling)
  ETHPROOFS_URL=               CLUSTER=  VM=ZisK  GUEST=monad-zisk  SOURCE=<hostname>

  ./witness-tap [--once] [--dry-run]

--dry-run never deletes and never writes to the queue; it prints what it would do. Use it first: this is
the only piece of the pipeline that removes files.
"""
import csv, json, os, re, socket, subprocess, sys, time, urllib.request

WITNESS_RE = re.compile(r"^(\d+)\.witness$")

def env(k, d=None):
    v = os.environ.get(k)
    return v if v not in (None, "") else d

HOME          = os.path.expanduser("~")
WITNESS_DIR   = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(env("WITNESS_DIR", f"{HOME}/witnesses")))
REPO          = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(env("REPO", f"{HOME}/zkvm-bench")))
BLOCK_DB      = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(env("BLOCK_DB", f"{HOME}/block_db")))
RPC           = env("RPC", "http://chi-001.devcore4.com:8545")
MANIFEST      = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(
                    env("MANIFEST", os.path.join(os.path.dirname(WITNESS_DIR), "witness-manifest.csv"))))
PROVE_EVERY = int(env("PROVE_EVERY", 1))     # 1 = every block (RTP). 100 = ethproofs sampling.
INTERVAL      = float(env("INTERVAL", 6))        # half a slot: never more than one block behind
# Retention policy (see the header): what makes a witness still worth its 7 MB.
KEEP_RECENT   = int(env("KEEP_RECENT", 10))      # newest N always kept — nothing in flight is removed
SKIP_AFTER    = int(env("SKIP_AFTER", 100))      # un-proved and this far behind -> abandoned, dropped
# WARM-UP: the first block a fresh producer emits is slower than the rest — it pays the one-time per-process
# costs (the KZG trusted setup on first use, a cold page cache, the db's first reads). With TIP_DIRECT=1 that
# is paid ONCE per producer, not per block, so it is a warm-up and not a cost of the block. Proving it anyway
# spends a prover slot on the slowest block of the run and, when the margin is thin, that lag never comes back.
# So the first WARMUP_BLOCKS selected blocks are produced (which is what warms the producer) and NOT queued.
# 0 disables it.
WARMUP_BLOCKS = int(env("WARMUP_BLOCKS", 1))
# Module level, so it survives every sweep of the loop: the budget is per PROCESS, not per cycle.
# A list because the sweep mutates it and rebinding a module global from inside would need `global`.
warmup_left = [WARMUP_BLOCKS]
STARTED_AT = time.time()   # anything older than this was produced by a previous, already-warm producer
KEEP_SAMPLE   = int(env("KEEP_SAMPLE", 1000))    # block % this == 0 -> kept forever (benchmark corpus)
# Calibrated on 191 real mainnet witnesses (2026-07-29): median 7.2 MB, p90 10.9, p99 13.9, max 15.6.
# A "keep forever" rule must stay near the p99 — at 12 MB it caught 7% of blocks, i.e. ~150 MB/h piling up.
KEEP_BIG_MB   = float(env("KEEP_BIG_MB", 14))    # witness this large -> kept (outlier worth profiling)
KEEP_SLOW_SECS = float(env("KEEP_SLOW_SECS", 45))  # proof took this long -> kept (outlier)
SETTLE_SECS   = float(env("SETTLE_SECS", 2))     # ignore a witness younger than this (still being written)
ETHPROOFS_URL = env("ETHPROOFS_URL")
CLUSTER       = env("CLUSTER")
VM            = env("VM", "ZisK")
GUEST         = env("GUEST", "monad-zisk")
SOURCE        = env("SOURCE", socket.gethostname().split(".")[0])
CHAIN_ID      = env("CHAIN_ID", "1")

QUEUE     = os.path.join(REPO, "guests", GUEST, "fixtures")
# Where a witness kept for PROFILING goes once it is out of the proving window. A subdirectory of the queue,
# because cli/prove-farm scans it with `find -maxdepth 1`: the files stay beside the queue they came from,
# stay findable by hand and by the profiling tools, and are invisible to the prover. The queue then means one
# thing only — blocks the prover should still attempt — which is what makes "consume the queue" a safe rule.
KEPT      = env("KEPT_DIR", os.path.join(QUEUE, "kept"))
GEN_INPUT = os.path.join(REPO, "guests", GUEST, "gen-inputs")
FIELDS    = ["block", "t_block", "t_avail", "t_witness", "t_queued", "bytes", "post_state_root", "promoted",
             "warmup", "block_hash"]
_manifest_fields = None      # the header the FILE actually has; resolved once per process

def log(msg):
    print(f"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S')} witness-tap: {msg}", flush=True)

def rpc(method, params):
    body = json.dumps({"jsonrpc": "2.0", "method": method, "params": params, "id": 1}).encode()
    req = urllib.request.Request(RPC, body, {"Content-Type": "application/json"})
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=15) as r:
        return json.load(r).get("result")

def chain_tip():
    """The REAL chain head (`latest`), not the finalized tag the producer follows — so the dashboard's
    lag includes the finality delay instead of hiding it."""
    try:
        return int(rpc("eth_blockNumber", []), 16)
    except Exception as e:                                    # noqa: BLE001 — never fatal
        log(f"warn: chain tip unavailable ({e})")
        return None

def block_header(n):
    """-> (timestamp, hash), both "" on failure. ONE RPC call for both: the response that carries the
    header timestamp carries the hash too, so recording the hash costs nothing."""
    try:
        b = rpc("eth_getBlockByNumber", [hex(n), False])
        if not b:
            return "", ""
        return int(b["timestamp"], 16), (b.get("hash") or "")
    except Exception:                                         # noqa: BLE001
        return "", ""

def block_db_mtime(n):
    for p in (os.path.join(BLOCK_DB, str(n)), os.path.join(BLOCK_DB, f"{n // 1_000_000}M", str(n))):
        try:
            return round(os.path.getmtime(p), 3)
        except OSError:
            continue
    return ""

def read_root(path):
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            return f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""

def seen_blocks():
    """The manifest IS the state: a block already recorded is never reprocessed (restart-safe)."""
    out = set()
    if os.path.exists(MANIFEST):
        with open(MANIFEST, newline="") as f:
            for row in csv.DictReader(f):
                try:
                    out.add(int(row["block"]))
                except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
                    pass
    return out

def manifest_last():
    """The highest block the manifest records, or None.

    THE DIRECTORY CANNOT ANSWER THIS. `last_witness` used to be max(WITNESS_DIR), and that dir is emptied by
    design — a raw witness is deleted the moment it is framed, precisely so the box does not hold 14 MB per
    block twice. What stays behind is whatever `keep_reason` retains for profiling, so the maximum was pinned
    to one old kept file: the heartbeat reported last_witness=25780229 for hours while the pipeline was at the
    tip, and the leaderboard showed a 1889-block lag on a pipeline with nothing wrong with it.

    The manifest is append-only and records every block processed, so its last line IS the last witness
    produced. Read backwards from the end: this runs every cycle, and parsing 27k rows to learn one number is
    work that grows with the run."""
    if not os.path.exists(MANIFEST):
        return None
    try:
        with open(MANIFEST, "rb") as f:
            f.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
            size = f.tell()
            back = min(size, 65536)
            f.seek(size - back)
            tail = f.read().decode(errors="replace").splitlines()
        for line in reversed(tail):
            first = line.split(",", 1)[0].strip()
            if first.isdigit():
                return int(first)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return None


def append_manifest(row):
    """Append against the header the file ACTUALLY has, not the one this version of the script wants.

    A manifest written before a column existed has no place for it, and csv.DictWriter RAISES on a key it
    does not know — which kills the tap outright rather than dropping a field. That is not hypothetical:
    adding `warmup` to the row without adding it here left the tap dead with
        ValueError: dict contains fields not in fieldnames: 'warmup'
    228 witnesses piled up in the dump, the prover idled for hours and the leaderboard stayed empty, all
    from one missing list entry. A row must never be able to kill the producer.

    Two guards, because either alone still loses something:
      * the header is read from the file, so a manifest from before the column keeps working unchanged;
      * extrasaction="ignore" drops any key that header lacks, so the NEXT column added here cannot
        repeat this even if someone forgets the file-header case.
    A new manifest gets FIELDS, so the column appears on the next rotation."""
    global _manifest_fields
    new = not os.path.exists(MANIFEST)
    if new:
        _manifest_fields = list(FIELDS)
    elif _manifest_fields is None:
        try:
            with open(MANIFEST, newline="") as f:
                hdr = next(csv.reader(f), None)
            _manifest_fields = hdr if hdr else list(FIELDS)
        except OSError:
            _manifest_fields = list(FIELDS)
        # And SAY when the file is missing a column, because the drop is otherwise invisible: downstream reads
        # an absent column as data the producer never had, not as a manifest that predates it. Once per process,
        # since the header is resolved once.
        missing = [c for c in FIELDS if c not in _manifest_fields]
        if missing:
            log(f"NOTE {MANIFEST} predates these columns and is NOT receiving them: {', '.join(missing)}. "
                f"Every column its header has is written normally. Move the file aside to start one that "
                f"carries them.")
    with open(MANIFEST, "a", newline="") as f:
        w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=_manifest_fields, extrasaction="ignore", restval="")
        if new:
            w.writeheader()
        w.writerow(row)

def safe_unlink(path, dry):
    """Only ever remove a <digits>.<witness|post_state_root> file INSIDE WITNESS_DIR. This is the one
    destructive operation in the pipeline; keep it boring and provable."""
    d, name = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)), os.path.basename(path)
    if d != WITNESS_DIR or not re.match(r"^\d+\.(witness|post_state_root)$", name):
        log(f"refusing to delete {path} (outside {WITNESS_DIR} or unexpected name)")
        return False
    if dry:
        log(f"[dry-run] would delete {name}")
        return True
    try:
        os.unlink(path)
        return True
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return True
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"warn: could not delete {name} ({e})")
        return False

def promote(block, wit, root_src, dry):
    """Frame the witness into the prover queue, reading it where it already is (--src-dir): one
    implementation of the ZisK input format (the guest's own gen-inputs), and no staging copy.

    This calls guests/<guest>/gen-inputs, which ONLY frames. It must never be switched to the stack
    dispatcher's near-identically named `run gen-input` verb: that one auto-profiles by default (it runs
    the zkVM emulation to write an exec-report next to the input, NO_REPORT=1 to skip), which would put an
    emulation on the producer's critical path for a work-unit the RTP pipeline never reads."""
    if dry:
        log(f"[dry-run] would queue {block} -> {QUEUE}/{CHAIN_ID}-{block}.bin")
        return ""
    r = subprocess.run([GEN_INPUT, "--src-dir", WITNESS_DIR, "--to", "fixtures", str(block)],
                       capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=REPO)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        log(f"ERROR: gen-inputs failed for {block}: {(r.stderr or r.stdout).strip().splitlines()[-1:]}")
        return ""
    return round(time.time(), 3)

def prove_status():
    """block -> (status, prove_secs) for THIS guest, read from run-data/prove-farm.csv — the same record
    its own is_proven() consults, so the tap and the prover never disagree about what is done."""
    out = {}
    path = os.path.join(REPO, "run-data", "prove-farm.csv")
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return out
    try:
        with open(path, newline="") as f:
            for row in csv.reader(f):
                # ts,block,guest,mode,status,prove_secs,total_secs,proof_bytes,run_dir
                if len(row) < 6 or row[2] != GUEST:
                    continue
                try:
                    b = int(row[1])
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                try:
                    secs = float(row[5])
                except ValueError:
                    secs = None
                prev = out.get(b)
                if prev is None or row[4] == "ok":        # an ok anywhere in the log wins
                    out[b] = (row[4], secs)
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"warn: cannot read prove-farm.csv ({e})")
    return out

def in_window(b, last):
    """Is this block still worth PROVING? Two decisions must not be confused: `keep_reason` says whether a
    file is worth its bytes (a huge block stays around for profiling long after the fact), while this says
    whether the prover should still consider it. Queueing an outlier from 280 blocks ago burns a proof slot
    on ancient history — the opposite of what --newest-first is for. Seen for real: blocks 25641069 and
    25641121 were proved while the tip was at 25641348, purely because they were kept as `big`."""
    return b > last - SKIP_AFTER

def keep_reason(b, last, size_bytes, status):
    """Why this block's witness is still worth keeping — or None if it can go. Order matters: the cheap,
    absolute rules first."""
    if b > last - KEEP_RECENT:
        return "recent"                                   # possibly in flight, never touch
    if KEEP_SAMPLE and b % KEEP_SAMPLE == 0:
        return "sample"                                   # the reproducible benchmark corpus
    st, secs = status.get(b, (None, None))
    if st is not None and st != "ok":
        return "proof-failed"                             # exactly what needs re-proving / debugging
    if size_bytes and size_bytes >= KEEP_BIG_MB * 1024 * 1024:
        return "big"                                      # heavy block: worth a deep profile
    if secs is not None and secs >= KEEP_SLOW_SECS:
        return "slow"                                     # slow proof: worth a deep profile
    if st == "ok":
        return None                                       # proved — the proof is the artefact now
    if b <= last - SKIP_AFTER:
        return None                                       # abandoned: --newest-first will never return
    return "unproved"

def queued_blocks():
    """Blocks that have a framed input, whether or not their raw witness still exists."""
    out = set()
    if not os.path.isdir(QUEUE):
        return out
    for n in os.listdir(QUEUE):
        m = re.match(rf"^{re.escape(CHAIN_ID)}-(\d+)\.bin$", n)
        if m:
            out.add(int(m.group(1)))
    return out

def prune(blocks, last, sizes, status, dry):
    """Apply the retention policy to BOTH the raw dump dir and the framed queue. Returns
    (dropped, abandoned, parked, kept_by_reason).

    The candidate set is the UNION of the two dirs, not just the dump dir: a framed input whose raw
    witness is already gone (the tap restarted, the dump dir was cleaned by hand) would otherwise never be
    re-examined, and cli/prove-farm would keep pulling ancient blocks out of the queue — the exact opposite
    of the RTP policy. Seen for real in a rehearsal: stale inputs 100 blocks behind the tip got proved."""
    dropped = abandoned = parked = 0
    kept = {}

    def park_queue_entry(b):
        """Move a kept-for-profiling input OUT of the queue. Returns how many files moved.

        Keeping a witness and offering it to the prover are two different decisions, and conflating them
        costs real proof slots: `in_window` already stops the tap from FRAMING an old block, but a block
        framed while it was recent and then kept as `big` stayed in the queue for ever, so --newest-first
        came back to it every time the queue drained. Seen twice — 25641069/25641121 proved at tip 25641348,
        and again on 2026-08-19 with blocks 355-375 behind the tip. Moving the file is what makes the two
        decisions physically separate instead of merely documented."""
        n = 0
        for suffix in (".bin", ".post_state_root"):
            src = os.path.join(QUEUE, f"{CHAIN_ID}-{b}{suffix}")
            if not os.path.exists(src):
                continue
            if dry:
                n += 1
                continue
            try:
                os.makedirs(KEPT, exist_ok=True)
                os.replace(src, os.path.join(KEPT, f"{CHAIN_ID}-{b}{suffix}"))
                n += 1
            except OSError as e:                              # noqa: BLE001 — never fatal
                log(f"warn: cannot park {os.path.basename(src)} ({e})")
        return n

    def drop_queue_entry(b):
        """Remove the framed input (never the .exec-report.json: it outlives the witness)."""
        n = 0
        for suffix in (".bin", ".post_state_root"):
            p = os.path.join(QUEUE, f"{CHAIN_ID}-{b}{suffix}")
            if not os.path.exists(p):
                continue
            if dry:
                log(f"[dry-run] would drop queue entry {os.path.basename(p)}")
            else:
                try:
                    os.unlink(p)
                except OSError as e:
                    log(f"warn: could not drop {os.path.basename(p)} ({e})")
                    continue
            n += 1 if suffix == ".bin" else 0
        return n

    # The FRAMED input is the artefact retention acts on — the raw witness is already gone by now (the
    # framed file contains it verbatim). So: a keep reason keeps the queue entry, no reason drops it.
    def bytes_of(b):
        """Witness size, from the raw file if it is still around, else recovered from the framed input
        (which is 8 bytes of length prefix + the witness + padding). Without this fallback the `big` rule
        could only ever fire on the cycle that framed the block, and an outlier would be dropped the moment
        it was proved."""
        if sizes.get(b) is not None:
            return sizes[b]
        try:
            return os.path.getsize(os.path.join(QUEUE, f"{CHAIN_ID}-{b}.bin")) - 8
        except OSError:
            return None

    for b in sorted(set(blocks) | queued_blocks()):
        reason = keep_reason(b, last, bytes_of(b), status)
        if reason:
            kept[reason] = kept.get(reason, 0) + 1
            # Worth its bytes, but no longer worth a proof slot: out of the queue it goes.
            if not in_window(b, last):
                parked += park_queue_entry(b)
            continue
        if status.get(b, (None, None))[0] != "ok":
            abandoned += 1
        # Safety net: a raw witness only survives to here if a cycle died between framing and deleting it.
        safe_unlink(os.path.join(WITNESS_DIR, f"{b}.witness"), dry)
        safe_unlink(os.path.join(WITNESS_DIR, f"{b}.post_state_root"), dry)
        drop_queue_entry(b)
        dropped += 1
    return dropped, abandoned, parked, kept

def queue_files():
    """How many framed inputs sit in the queue.

    Must match the exact tag form, NOT just "*.bin": the queue also holds `<tag>.pv.bin` public values
    (execute_local writes its artefacts next to the input), and counting those inflated the number this
    reports to the dashboard. cli/prove-farm and witness-profile both filter on the strict form already.

    NOT the un-proven backlog either: prove-farm leaves an input in place until retention drops it. The
    meaningful backlog signal is the LAG between last_witness and the last proved block."""
    return len(queued_blocks()) if os.path.isdir(QUEUE) else 0

def heartbeat(tip, last_witness):
    """Feed cli/ethproofs-mock's POST /status — the producer is the only party that knows the tip."""
    if not ETHPROOFS_URL:
        return
    body = {"chain_tip": tip, "last_witness": last_witness, "queue_files": queue_files(),
            "vm": VM, "guest": GUEST, "source": SOURCE}
    if CLUSTER:
        body["cluster_id"] = CLUSTER
    try:
        req = urllib.request.Request(ETHPROOFS_URL.rstrip("/") + "/status",
                                     json.dumps(body).encode(), {"Content-Type": "application/json"})
        urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=10).read()
    except Exception as e:                                    # noqa: BLE001 — a dashboard, never fatal
        log(f"warn: heartbeat failed ({e})")

def cycle(seen, dry):
    try:
        names = os.listdir(WITNESS_DIR)
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"ERROR: cannot read {WITNESS_DIR} ({e})")
        return
    blocks = sorted(int(m.group(1)) for m in (WITNESS_RE.match(n) for n in names) if m)
    # An EMPTY dir is the healthy steady state, not a reason to skip the cycle: returning here also skipped the
    # heartbeat, so the leaderboard went silent exactly when the tap was keeping up perfectly with the tip.
    # `last` comes from the manifest either way — see manifest_last() for why the directory cannot answer it.
    last = manifest_last()
    if last is None:
        if not blocks:
            return
        last = blocks[-1]
    promoted = stale = 0
    sizes = {}
    # Retention needs to be decided BEFORE framing: on a cold start with a backlog (the replay catching up
    # after a restart), framing every block and then deleting most of them would write over a gigabyte for
    # nothing. So read the prover's record up front.
    status = prove_status()

    for b in blocks:
        wit  = os.path.join(WITNESS_DIR, f"{b}.witness")
        rootf = os.path.join(WITNESS_DIR, f"{b}.post_state_root")
        is_cadence = (b % PROVE_EVERY == 0)
        # Consume the warm-up budget only on a witness written AFTER this process started. A backlog from the
        # previous producer is already warm, so burning coverage on it would warm nothing — the whole point is
        # to absorb the first block of the CURRENT producer. Restarting the tap alone (a supervisor restart,
        # say) against an already-warm producer therefore costs one block; that is the price of not needing
        # the tap and the producer to agree on who started when.
        # Consumed by the FIRST post-start block, cadence or not. The one-time cost lands on whichever
        # block the producer emits first; if that block is off-cadence it was never going to be queued, so
        # the warm-up already happened for free and the budget must be spent there rather than held. Holding
        # it would burn the next real candidate for a producer that is warm by then: at PROVE_EVERY=5 from
        # block 12 that cost block 15 and bought nothing.
        # THE CADENCE IS NEVER SHIFTED. Selection stays `b % PROVE_EVERY == 0` on the absolute block number,
        # so a warm-up at 10 resumes at 15, not at 11 — the same blocks are picked across runs and zkVMs,
        # which is what makes a cross-prover comparison possible at all.
        warmup = False
        if warmup_left[0] > 0:
            try:
                if os.path.getmtime(wit) >= STARTED_AT:
                    warmup_left[0] -= 1
                    warmup = is_cadence          # only a cadence block had something to suppress
                    if is_cadence:
                        is_cadence = False
                        log(f"block {b}: warm-up, produced but NOT queued "
                            f"({warmup_left[0]} left)")
                    else:
                        log(f"block {b}: warm-up absorbed by an off-cadence block, no coverage spent "
                            f"({warmup_left[0]} left)")
            except OSError:
                pass
        try:
            sizes[b] = os.path.getsize(wit)
        except OSError:
            pass

        # Never touch a witness the replay may still be writing. Framing a truncated one is UNRECOVERABLE:
        # the raw copy that could be re-framed from is deleted as soon as the block is framed, so there is
        # nothing to fall back on. Anything younger than SETTLE_SECS is simply left for the next cycle.
        try:
            if time.time() - os.path.getmtime(wit) < SETTLE_SECS:
                continue
        except OSError:
            continue

        if b not in seen:
            t_queued = ""
            if is_cadence and not in_window(b, last):
                # Too far behind to ever be proved, whether or not we keep the witness. Record it (the
                # coverage denominator must include it) but spend nothing on framing it.
                stale += 1
            elif is_cadence:
                t_queued = promote(b, wit, rootf, dry)
                # Count the would-be work in a dry run too, else it always reports queued=0 and the
                # preview looks like it does nothing.
                promoted += 1 if (t_queued != "" or dry) else 0
                if t_queued == "" and not dry:
                    # Framing failed: leave the raw witness, record nothing, retry next cycle. Writing a
                    # manifest row here would mark the block seen and it would never come back.
                    continue
            # A dry run must NOT record anything: a recorded block is never reprocessed, so writing the
            # manifest here would make the following real run skip the promotion it just previewed.
            if dry:
                log(f"[dry-run] would record block {b} (promoted={1 if is_cadence else 0})")
            else:
                t_blk, blk_hash = block_header(b) if is_cadence else ("", "")
                append_manifest({
                    "block": b,
                    "t_block": t_blk,                                # 1 RPC call per queued block (~1/slot)
                    "t_avail": block_db_mtime(b),
                    "t_witness": round(os.path.getmtime(wit), 3) if os.path.exists(wit) else "",
                    "t_queued": t_queued,
                    "bytes": os.path.getsize(wit) if os.path.exists(wit) else "",
                    "post_state_root": read_root(rootf),
                    "promoted": 1 if t_queued != "" else 0,
                    # Recorded so the coverage denominator stays honest AND a 0 here is explicable: a
                    # warm-up block was never a candidate, it is not a block the prover missed.
                    "warmup": 1 if warmup else 0,
                    # The third public value the guest commits. post_state_root anchors the state and the
                    # parent's row anchors the pre-state, so writing the hash here completes the ground truth
                    # cli/check-pv needs — and completes it LOCALLY, so the submission path verifies every
                    # value without a single RPC call on the hot path.
                    "block_hash": blk_hash,
                })
                seen.add(b)
                # The raw witness is now redundant, so it goes immediately — this is the whole point: the
                # framed input IS the witness (LE64 len + witness + pad), so nothing is lost and the box
                # stops holding 14 MB per block across two directories. A block that was deliberately not
                # framed (out of window, or off-cadence) has no future use either, so its raw goes too.
                safe_unlink(wit, dry)
                safe_unlink(rootf, dry)


    # Retention runs over everything present, driven by what the prover has actually done.
    dropped, abandoned, parked, kept = prune(blocks, last, sizes, status, dry)
    raw_left = len([n for n in os.listdir(WITNESS_DIR) if WITNESS_RE.match(n)]) if os.path.isdir(WITNESS_DIR) else 0

    tip = chain_tip()
    lag = (tip - last) if isinstance(tip, int) else "?"
    kept_str = " ".join(f"{k}={v}" for k, v in sorted(kept.items())) or "-"
    # raw= should hover at 0-1: the dump dir is drained every cycle, so anything piling up there means
    # framing is failing or the replay is outrunning the tap.
    log(f"{'[dry-run] ' if dry else ''}last_witness={last} tip={tip} lag={lag} queued={promoted}"
        f"{f' stale={stale}' if stale else ''} dropped={dropped}"
        f"{f' (abandoned {abandoned})' if abandoned else ''}"
        f"{f' parked={parked}' if parked else ''} kept[{kept_str}] "
        f"queue={queue_files()} raw={raw_left}")
    # No heartbeat on a dry run: "dry" has to mean no side effects anywhere, including the dashboard.
    if not dry:
        heartbeat(tip, last)

def main():
    dry = "--dry-run" in sys.argv
    once = "--once" in sys.argv
    for a in sys.argv[1:]:
        if a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__)
            return
        if a not in ("--dry-run", "--once"):
            sys.exit(f"witness-tap: unknown argument {a}\n{__doc__}")
    if not os.path.isdir(WITNESS_DIR):
        sys.exit(f"witness-tap: no witness dir at {WITNESS_DIR} (set WITNESS_DIR=…)")
    if not os.access(GEN_INPUT, os.X_OK):
        sys.exit(f"witness-tap: {GEN_INPUT} not found/executable (is REPO={REPO} the zkvm-bench clone?)")
    log(f"dir={WITNESS_DIR} repo={REPO} prove_every={PROVE_EVERY} keep_recent={KEEP_RECENT} "
        f"warmup={WARMUP_BLOCKS} "
        f"manifest={MANIFEST}{' [DRY-RUN]' if dry else ''}{' [once]' if once else ''}"
        f"{f' heartbeat={ETHPROOFS_URL}' if ETHPROOFS_URL else ''}")
    seen = seen_blocks()
    log(f"{len(seen)} block(s) already in the manifest")
    while True:
        cycle(seen, dry)
        if once:
            return
        time.sleep(INTERVAL)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        main()
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        log("stopped.")
